=== 4. Credential guardrail (Done-when box 3) ===
- Password literal never appears in db/seed/seed-data.json (reference data
  only - categories/brands/units/products/suppliers, no Users).
- Passwords are generated at runtime in SeedCommand.GenerateInstallationPassword
  (RandomNumberGenerator, 24 chars, same shape as bootstrap.ps1's
  New-InstallationPassword) and held in memory only until printed/appended.
- G-C (scripts/check-no-csharp.ps1) scans client projects for credential
  patterns; this card touches none of them. Full guardrail run below is
  green regardless.
- installation-credentials.txt lives under the ACL-protected ConfigRoot
  bootstrap.ps1's own step 4 already locks to Administrators/SYSTEM only -
  the seed command appends to the SAME file rather than writing a second
  one, so an operator has exactly one place to look.

=== 5. bootstrap.ps1 wiring ===
scripts/bootstrap.ps1 step 9 runs
  dotnet run --project src/Merchandising.Maintenance -- seed
immediately after step 8 (certificate), using the same repoRoot working
directory the migrate step (step 5) already relies on for its default
db/migrations path - "seed" resolves db/seed/seed-data.json the same way.
Verified by inspection and by the manual runs in sections 1-2 above, which
exercise the exact command bootstrap.ps1 invokes; the full bootstrap.ps1
run itself was not re-executed end-to-end for this evidence capture because
this machine already holds a live installation and -Force would rotate the
three database identities' passwords on a database other tasks' evidence
depends on - a destructive action outside this card's scope. Steps 1-8 were
already proven in Phase 1/P2-10 evidence; step 9 is proven directly here.

